.TH BASE32 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
base32 \- encode or decode data in base32
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B base32
.RB [ \-di ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR cols ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B base32
encodes
.I file
in base32, as defined by RFC 4648, or with
.B \-d
decodes it, and writes the result to standard output.
With no
.IR file ,
or when it is
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
Encoding turns each 5 octets into 8 characters from the alphabet
.B A
to
.B Z
and
.B 2
to
.BR 7 ,
and pads the last group to 8 characters with
.BR = .
The output is broken into lines of 76 characters, the last of which may
be shorter, and each line ends with a newline.
Empty input produces no output.
.PP
Decoding accepts letters in either case.
Whitespace, control characters, and other characters outside the
alphabet are ignored, so line breaks and spaces may appear anywhere.
Padding is required.
When the input ends inside a group of 8 characters,
.B base32
writes the octets of the complete groups, reports
.BR "truncated input" ,
and exits with status 1.
Decoding continues after padding, so encodings joined with line breaks
between them decode as one.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped and may follow
.IR file .
The argument of
.B \-w
may be attached, as in
.BR \-w0 ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-d
Decode instead of encode.
.TP
.B \-i
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
Decoding always ignores characters outside the alphabet.
.TP
.BI "\-w " cols
Break encoded output into lines of
.I cols
characters instead of 76.
A
.I cols
of 0 writes no line breaks at all, not even a final newline.
.I cols
is a decimal number up to 2147483647.
Ignored when decoding.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, options must come before
.IR file .
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid option or
.IR cols ,
more than one
.IR file ,
.I file
could not be opened or read, truncated input when decoding, or a write
error.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader exited before
.B base32
finished writing.
When this is first detected as the output is flushed at exit, the
status is 1 instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encode a string:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf hello | base32
NBSWY3DP
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode a key written in groups, and show it in hex:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aqJBSW Y3DP EHPK 3PXP\(aq | base32 \-d | xxd \-p
48656c6c6f21deadbeef
.EE
.RE
.PP
Generate a random 160-bit secret:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ head \-c 20 /dev/urandom | base32
.EE
.RE
.PP
Encode a file on a single line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ base32 \-w0 key.bin >key.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR base32 :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options, such as
.B \-\-decode
or
.BR \-\-wrap .
.IP \(bu 2
Decoding accepts lowercase letters.
.IP \(bu 2
Decoding ignores characters outside the alphabet even without
.BR \-i .
.PP
Decoding works through the input in pieces that end at a character with
code 32 (space) or less, or after 80 characters, which causes two bugs:
.IP \(bu 2
A character outside the alphabet with a code above 32, inside a group of
8 that continues into the next piece, shifts the decoding, which
corrupts the output or ends in truncated input.
.IP \(bu 2
Data after padding in the last piece is reported as truncated input,
as in
.B MZXW6===MZXW6===
with no final newline.
.PP
Standard input and output are binary.
Encoded lines end with LF, and carriage returns in encoded input are
ignored, so CRLF line endings decode correctly.
Decoded octets reach a file or pipe unchanged, but when standard output
is a console they pass through the busybox-w32 console layer as text:
escape sequences take effect, non-ASCII bytes may be converted, and NUL
bytes cut the output short.
.PP
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
a device name such as
.IR /dev/null ;
see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR base64 (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR uuencode (1),
.BR xxd (1)
